    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:ec46e3209ab74818",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "c9a70e5d77a2cb45001f5e5e1f7e7b335033f261792cf776473f0d5d75f53290",
      "text": "After breakfast, head to the Valdés Peninsula in Chubut Province, in central Patagonia. The peninsula has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1999.\n\nFew people live on Valdés, and its waters and shores are home to many marine animals and birds. You may see southern right whales, fur seals, elephant seals, orcas and rare dolphins with distinctive markings.\n\nA narrow strip of land connects the peninsula to the mainland, between the Nuevo and San José gulfs.\n\nThe next part of the excursion starts in Puerto Pirámides. From there, you’ll take a boat trip of about 1.5 hours to watch for whales. They come to the peninsula from July to December to calve, and sometimes come very close to the boats.\n\nThe peninsula has salt lakes, cliffs, beaches and reefs. On the coast, you can get close to areas where elephant seals gather.\n\nLunch is at San Lorenzo. Afterwards, visit a reserve with a colony of Magellanic penguins. They come ashore, where you can watch them from close range.\n\nReturn to Puerto Madryn after the excursion.\n\n**Duration:** full day.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-mw96l01v-myqa6co0d78doi5ozugapb",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      }
    },
